On Aug 25, 11:56=A0am, Andrea <ene...@gmail.com> wrote:
> So ideally I'd like to turn off random monster generation and random
> monster-inventory generation for my levels... =A0Any number I can easily
> change to nerf the probability, for example?

     You could prevent new monsters from being created
once the level has been populated by adding

  if (Is_stronghold(&u.uz) && !in_mklev) return 0;

to the beginning of function makemon() in makemon.c, so
that monster creation attempts fail.  Is_stronghold(&u.uz)
tests for being on the Castle level; in_mklev is only true
while a level is being created and !in_mklev negates that,
so yields true once the level has finished being created.

     That will stop _all_ new monsters on that level:
randomly created ones which ordinarily occur every N turns,
scrolls of create monster read by the hero or by existing
monsters, quaffed milky or smoky potions releasing ghosts
or djinn instead of producing the potion effect, splitting
long worms, cloning grelims or assorted fungi, hatching
eggs, applying figurines to activate them, zapping corpses
with undead turning or statues with stone-to-flesh, spell
casters summoning swarms of monsters, and doubtlessly other
methods which escape me at the moment.  Trying to block
those events piecemeal would take a substantial effort;
blocking them all at once is simple, but does mean that a
lot of things in nethack won't work as normal on that level.

     Something comparable could be done for objects, but a
blanket rejection of new items seems more likely to stand out
to the player that something has been broken.  Also there are
probably places in the code that assume object creation will
always succeed (failure due to memory depletion already kills
the program, so the rest of the program doesn't worry about
that contingency) and could end up crashing if a null object
gets produced.  Monster generation is always ready to deal
with the chance that no monster gets produced, but object
generation is not as rigorous about that.


